255;175;215m⠉⠁[0m[4B[2A[2C[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[21A[J[38;2;124;124;124mInteresting! For odd n, B's first response: if A picks 1, B picks n; otherwise B[0m[38;2;124;124;124mpicks 1. (By symmetry, if A picks n, B should pick 1 — yes. And if A picks 1, B [0m[38;2;124;124;124mpicks n. So B "completes the endpoint pair": B picks the endpoint far from A, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124munless A picked 1 in which case B picks n... wait that's the same. B picks the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mOPPOSITE endpoint. If A picks 1, B picks n. If A picks anything else (including [0m[38;2;124;124;124mn), B picks 1.)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, actually: A=1→B=n; A=n→B=1; A=other→B=1. So B picks 1 unless A picked 1 [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(then B picks n). Equivalently: B picks an endpoint (1 or n) different from A's [0m[38;2;124;124;124mmove if A picked an endpoint; if A didn't pick an endpoint, B picks 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually simpler: B always picks 1, unless A picked 1 (then B picks n). I.e., B [0m[38;2;124;124;124mpicks the smallest available endpoint, preferring 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, but why not symmetric? If A picks n, B picks 1 (not n-1 or something). And[0m[38;2;124;124;124mif A picks 1, B picks n. So B picks the endpoint NOT chosen by A, when A chose [0m
[38;2;124;124;124man endpoint; and picks 1 when A chose an interior point.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, actually it's: B picks 1 if available, else n. Since if A picked 1, then 1 [0m[38;2;124;124;124mnot available, B picks n. If A picked anything else, 1 is available, B picks 1. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mThat's the rule! "B picks 1 if available, otherwise n."[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me verify: A=1→1 not available→B=n. ✓. A=2→1 available→B=1. ✓. A=n→1 [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mavailable→B=1. ✓.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo B's first move: pick 1 if available, else n. I.e., B grabs the leftmost [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mendpoint (or rightmost if leftmost is taken).[0m

