[38;2;124;124;124mLet me reconsider the parity-breaking insight more carefully and try to build a [0m[38;2;124;124;124mstrategy around it.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor even n, A's first move a with parity p. For a draw, A must collect all of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mparity p (the parity of a), and B all of parity 1-p. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mB's goal: (1) break the draw by taking a number of parity p (so A can't complete[0m[38;2;124;124;124mparity p), AND (2) trap A.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mB's first move: take an endpoint of parity 1-p (B's parity for the draw). This [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mis consistent with B collecting parity 1-p. So B's first move doesn't break the [0m[38;2;124;124;124mdraw; it's "cooperating" with the draw parity. Then B's SECOND move breaks the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdraw by taking a number of parity p.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor n=8, A=1 (p=odd): B's parity for draw = even. B takes 8 (even, endpoint) — [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcooperating. Then B takes 5 (odd = p) — breaking! Then trap.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor n=8, A=2 (p=even): B's parity for draw = odd. B takes 1 (odd, endpoint) — [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcooperating. Then B takes 3 (odd) — still cooperating?! Trace: A=2,B=1,A=4,B=3. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mB=3 is odd (cooperating). Then A=6, B=8 (even = p!) — breaking on move 3. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the parity-breaking move timing varies. For A=1, B breaks on move 2 (takes 5,[0m[38;2;124;124;124modd). For A=2, B breaks on move 3 (takes 8, even). [0m

